§ feedback / rqmts

☑ search — the $.field query language now works in the TABLE, not just the
  inspector: a field query annotates rather than hides, driving the match
  counts below. Plain-text still filters connections by URL.
☑ aggregation — bandwidth is the default per-node aggregate (↑/↓ on ALL +
  every process/container header); the agg button / m toggles bandwidth ⇄
  message count. (controls.aggMetric, components/agg.jsx)
☑ error detection — done via SEARCH as suggested, not a bespoke feature: a
  $.field query (e.g. $.error) turns the per-node aggregate into matching /
  total messages, live, flagged red when >0 — per service AND per connection
  row. So "show error per service" = type $.error and watch the counts.

¶ These are ecs services btw (relevant to the egress-cost concern above).

☑ hardcoded support for standard binaries — no --bin/--pid now
  auto-discovers a running node/deno/bun/python/ruby and traces its SSL
  binary; --bin is only for extras. (probes/probe.js knownRuntimeBin /
  KNOWN_BINS)

§ TLS-library coverage (outbound wss:// clients)

¶ The fanout stack dials wss:// out as a CLIENT, so TLS is in-process and
  the socket-layer plaintext tap sees only ciphertext — coverage is per TLS
  library, at the SSL_read/SSL_write boundary. One item each. (The plaintext
  tap already covers the ALB-terminated INBOUND leg for every language;
  these are the outbound-client gaps.) Write it generally, not privileging
  this set.

☐ Python websockets — confirm the OpenSSL path with a client demo (high)
  stdlib ssl → dynamic libssl.so, python3 auto-discovered; should already
  work (the sweet spot). Add an outbound wss:// python client to the demo
  and verify decode end to end.
☐ Rust tokio-tungstenite — tap native-tls, detect and flag rustls (med)
  native-tls → OpenSSL (tappable, dynamic or vendored-static); rustls → pure
  Rust, no SSL_read/write (blind). Indistinguishable by name — scan the
  pid's maps/exe for libssl or an SSL_read symbol; tap native-tls, mark
  rustls opaque.
☐ C++ uWebSockets/uSockets — attach to static OpenSSL/BoringSSL in the exe
  (med) Vendored, usually statically linked into the app exe; tappable iff
  SSL_read/SSL_write survive (release/stripped builds often localize them).
  Needs exe symbol resolution; BoringSSL uses the same symbol names.
☐ Go gorilla/websocket — Go-specific uprobes, a separate feature (low ugh)
  Pure-Go crypto/tls, no OpenSSL symbols at all — blind to the SSL uprobe
  regardless of role. Needs uprobes on crypto/tls.(*Conn).Read/Write with
  the Go ABI + Go symbol table (uretprobe/arg handling differ). Large,
  standalone; flag Go connections as opaque until then.

☑ Enabler: classify a process by SSL reachability, not the KNOWN_BINS name
  list — so it privileges no runtime. discover.sslClass returns libssl (maps
  a libssl, any language) / runtime (static-OpenSSL exe, name hint) /
  opaque; procinfo.classifySsl caches it per pid (raced maps query); the
  browser gates and labels on it. A Rust native-tls or dynamically-linked
  C++ app now shows up automatically; Go/rustls fall out as opaque.

¶ Still blocked, the static-symbol half: an exe that statically links
  OpenSSL but isn't a known runtime reads as opaque — the graph exposes maps
  + exe but no ELF symbol table, so "does the exe export SSL_read" isn't
  answerable client-side. Needs a daemon symbol field or an attach-and-see
  probe.

